it's great to be joined today by Stephen  Schwartz who is a scientist futurist and  also award-winning author he's a  columnist for the journal Explorer  editor of the daily shorts report net  and also distinguished consulting  faculty of Saybrook University  Stephan it's so interesting to speak to  you today and have been following your  work for some time and a lot of your  work that I'm very interested in relates  to social change and the role that  individuals can play in catalyzing real  and significant social change so first  just sort of to get us into this topic  talk to us a little bit about the  changes that you've seen and  participated in personally really I  think dating back to the 1960s well I  got interested in this because when I  was a teenager back in the late 50s  early 60s I got involved with the civil  rights movement and the four times in my  life I've been involved with changing  history in a life-affirming way in the  50s and 60s it was civil rights in the  70s I was this special assistant to the  Chief of Naval Operations Elmo Zumwalt  was then CNO and we were involved with  transforming the military from an  elitist conscription organization to an  all-volunteer meritocracy the military  we have today and then in the 80s I  along with a group from Esalen Institute  we started what came to be known as  citizen diplomacy creating a  back-channel between the Soviet Union in  the United States because we were afraid  we were going to have a nuclear war and  then through all of that what I think of  is the consciousness movement and its  first cousin the environmental movement  the idea that we are more than animated  meat and that all life is interconnected  and interdependent and about 16 or no  almost 18 years ago now I began doing  really serious research  looking at how individuals and small  groups can change the arc of history in  a life-affirming way so that's where I  want to start digging in with you a  little bit I mean in the various  instances that you've observed or  participated in significant social  transformations have you identified any  common characteristics in terms of the  involvement of the individual in  catalyzing those changes yes yeah that's  a very good question in fact that was  the point I recently wrote a book called  the eight laws of change which is  basically exactly how to do this so that  there's two stages or two levels of it I  guess the first is the individual level  and then there is this social group  level at the individual level it's  really remarkably simple and it doesn't  require a lot of money or doesn't  require official position or armies or  anything like that and it's simply that  whenever you are faced with the choice  and we do thousands of them every day  you know you go into a store to buy  toothpaste or cat food or something  that's really a vote in a sense and so  what you do is I call it the quotidian  choice which just means daily or mundane  everyday you make dozens and dozens of  these choices and you consistently  choose two of the options available to  you to always choose the one that is the  most compassionate and life-affirming as  you understand it at that moment so you  know you when you go in to buy the  toothpaste you look at the toothpaste  and think is this does this company  treat its workers well is it  environmentally sensitive you make  choices that you are going to make  anyway but you always make them leaning  towards or with a bias towards that  which is compassionate and  life-affirming and when this happens  when a group of people join together and  do this then you see real cultural  change  that occurs examples of that would be  well you don't see people out of people  smoking anymore nobody passed a law  against it it occurred because thousands  and thousands of people made it an  individual daily decision when you when  you look at for instance the transition  from gay to LGBT which is not just a  transition of a different term but also  a different way of conceptualizing  gender you can see that in a very short  space of time  we went from saying gay to seeing LGBT  and now it's LGBTQ because we are going  through a cultural reappraisal of what  gender means to us as as a people or  anything so you don't pretty so then you  don't there are some who believe that  that change you just identified is a  change in language and not a change in  anything else and that thus we should  not be quote policing language however  you actually believe aside from whatever  your views are about policing language  that that linguistic change does  correlate with a societal change well of  course it doesn't mean look at the  transition that marriage equality was  unthinkable during the Clinton  administration and now today if you talk  to Millennials or or what are now called  VI gens those born after 95 to them it's  just perfectly normal another example  you used to never see um interracial  romances in movies and television now if  you look at Netflix or HBO or stars as a  series they run you see that interracial  romance is a big feature because the  culture has changed not because the the  Congress or the president made some sort  of pronouncement or passed a law but  because ordinary individuals simply made  choice the power of collective intention  is the most powerful social force on  earth so Stephen when it comes to trying  to catalyze significant societal change  starting at the individual level and you  feel that none or neither of the choices  you have are particularly good I'd like  to talk a little bit about how you might  direct people to act in those ways and  just as one example you know that  there's I think it's easy to think about  this um in terms of Elections there were  a lot of people in the recent election  who identified both Donald Trump and  Hillary Clinton of fundamentally being  part of the same sort of establishment  crony capitalist status quo that they  don't like so there were some who said  well I see one candidate is less bad  than the other thus I will vote for them  and there were people who chose to  abstain altogether with the idea that  listen these two people are so close it  doesn't matter if one slightly worse I  want to burn the entire system down to  rebuild it from the ground up your  philosophy which one of those two  approaches or some other would it  support in the context of all choices  that aren't very good well here's the  key David I don't really care about  politics except anthropologically okay I  I don't want to get lost in the various  theories or blow the Asians or polemical  you know diatribes that that did this  sort of language in the talking part  well but let's apply it let's apply it  then for some other example where none  of our choices are particularly good  okay let me finish the thought instead  what I look at is social outcomes  because that's an objectively verifiable  measure you know if you do this what  happens well if you look at the world  from that point of view where if you  create this policy this is what happens  then all of this becomes much clearer so  you can look let's just take the Hillary  Trump thing Hillary Clinton may have  been a deeply flawed candidate Donald  Trump at least from my perspective on  the on the basis of his 200 days of  performance  so far has been a disaster so in terms  of social I've come down and neither one  of them was a good choice but I mean I  was a Bernie Sanders candidate but given  if you just had that choice that that  that binary choice on the basis of  social outcome data you should have  voted for Hillary Clinton because she  may be a flawed candidate but she is a  better candidate than Donald Trump on  the basis not of political philosophy or  Benghazi or the usual nonsense yeah that  eats up all the oxygen but on the basis  of performance she has a long history of  gender equality she has a long history  of looking for universal health care she  has a long history of gender equality so  on the basis just of those three things  like to pick a dozen more she may not  have been a great candidate but compared  to Donald Trump she was the better  candidate you don't always get to make  perfect choices a lot of times the  choices available to you none of them  are really very good but if you look at  them closely and you look at the social  outcome data you will see that there's  always one that's a little bit better  than the other and that's the one you  always want to choose it's not perfect  but it's better and it's the long-term  of that that makes the difference not  not our focus today but on one just one  quick thing you said I don't believe  Hillary Clinton was in favor of  single-payer health care but that's it's  not relevant to our conversation but  just just as one note it heard go back  and look at her health care failed  health care problem or policy program  when Clinton was president and you will  see that she was building the basis for  a single-payer health care okay I will  review that when it comes to violent and  nonviolent change Steven you've pointed  out that violent or coercive change in  society seems not to stick around nearly  as long as nonviolent change can you  give us some examples of that and maybe  talk about the underlying reasons why  that  might be yes actually that was one of  the most interesting things I discovered  when I was started doing this research  you know if you if you look at change  they're basically two ways change occurs  the the one that we glorify and put  statues in the park to is some violent  change but in fact if you looked at the  research of a number of scientists and  as well as my own research and and what  I am indeed social transformations  around the world from the beginning of  the 20th century to 2014 and and of  those 200 plus I've forgotten exactly  but a little over 200 what you see is  that 75 percent of those which are  involved 